1. Difference between Frontend, Backend, and Full-Stack Development

Frontend Development:
The frontend is the part of a website users see and interact with directly — everything displayed in the browser such as text, buttons, images, and forms.
Technologies: HTML, CSS, JavaScript, React, Angular.
Example: The login form, navigation bar, and animations on Flipkart’s homepage.

Backend Development:
The backend works behind the scenes — it handles logic, database interactions, authentication, and communication between the client and server.
Technologies: Node.js, Python (Django/Flask), Java (Spring Boot), PHP.
Example: When you log in, the backend verifies your credentials in the database and sends a response.

Full-Stack Development:
A full-stack developer works on both frontend and backend parts of a web application.
Example: A developer building both the user interface and the server logic of a food delivery app.

