3. How a Browser Requests and Displays a Web Page

User enters a URL (e.g., www.google.com) in the browser.

The browser sends a DNS request to find the IP address of the server.

The browser sends an HTTP/HTTPS request to the web server.

The server processes the request, fetches data (if needed) from the database, and sends back an HTML response.

The browser receives the response, downloads linked CSS, JS, and images.

The rendering engine parses HTML, applies styles, runs scripts, and finally displays the complete webpage to the user.

